From b816f1fc6ef7adb5db8533cf22acdc7ce362d37c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:35:37 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: settings for the engine of native Dolby Vision
 output

The automatic choices of native Dolby Vision output (the drawing path, frames presented a frame ahead,
the fidelity step-down) could only be overridden from advancedsettings.xml, and the composition and the
keeping of repeated pictures not from the settings at all. Measuring them on a GPU, or comparing them on
a display, meant editing a file. A group "Dolby Vision engine" under System > Display now holds them,
each one Automatic by default, which is what native output did without it. Frame ahead and the
step-down are at the level of the booleans they take over (advanced), so that nobody who saw those
loses them; the other three are for experts (a group of Kodi's settings has no level of its own: it is
shown with any setting of it that is):
 - Drawing path (videoscreen.hadesdvpathmode): automatic, always direct, always composed. Frames with GUI
   elements, and pictures that do not fill the screen, are composed whatever it says. With a way set,
   CDVPathChoice::SetMode() makes the choice give that way and leaves everything it knows as it was:
   nothing that is measured meanwhile reaches what it keeps for later sessions (videoscreen.hadesdvpath),
   and set back to automatic it decides as if those frames had not been drawn.
 - Present frames a frame ahead (videoscreen.hadesdvaheadmode): when needed, always, never. It takes over
   the boolean videoscreen.hadesdvahead: off becomes never; <dvaheadalways> still turns "when needed"
   into "always", and "never" stays the user's, as before.
 - Fidelity step-down (videoscreen.hadesdvstepmode): automatic, never, level 1, 2, 3. It takes over
   videoscreen.hadesdvstepdown: off becomes never; <dvstepdown> still comes first. A set level is drawn
   as the frame's ladder takes it (CDVStepDown::Effective()): a level that changes nothing for a frame
   (dvbridge_render_step_levels(): with the composer of CCM 001, levels 1 and 2 without the residual of
   an enhancement layer; with libplacebo's reshaping, levels 2 and 3) draws the picture of the highest
   level under it that changes it, or the exact one, and the frame is drawn, and told, at that level.
   The same bytes; the level that the log and the player information give is the one the picture is.
   This applies to <dvstepdown> too.
 - Composition (videoscreen.hadesdvcomposer): as the specification (ETSI GS CCM 001), or libplacebo's
   reshaping. Read when native output starts and when a bridge for output as HDR10 is made, and given to
   the renderer (CDVBridgeGLES::UseComposer(), dvbridge_render_use_ccm_composer()), which a renderer made
   later takes too (UseComposerSetting(), which no longer reads advancedsettings.xml itself);
   <dvccmcomposer>false still asks for libplacebo's reshaping.
 - Keep repeated pictures (videoscreen.hadesdvkeeprepeats): on; off draws every refresh again, as
   <dvkeeprepeats>false does, which still turns it off too.

Every one is read when native output starts (DVEngineRead()), never in the middle of a video, and the log
tells them the first time and whenever they change. What an automatic choice finds while a setting makes
the frames other work than it would choose is not kept: the step-down measures on a copy while the way
is set (its windows are of a way), the way of drawing measures on a copy while a level is set, and both do
with libplacebo's composition (CDVLearned, dropped when the output ends).

The two booleans stay, hidden: they are what an earlier build reads, and they follow the settings that
took them over (on for every mode but "never"; CDVEngineSettings::Migrate(), when the settings are loaded
and whenever the new ones change), so a user's choice survives the update and the way back.

Defaults, and the migrated booleans with every advancedsettings.xml override, decide what the code before this commit
decided (host test). The strings: core 39950, 39964-39977, and 39962, 39963, 39987 and 39988 reworded
for the settings that took them over; Estuary 31900-31914 and the four that mirror those, in English and
the three Spanish ones.
